WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.120
﻿SIERRA: Thoughts about Africa can fall into&nbsp;
cliches. There’s the romanticized image of endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.960
natural beauty - waterfalls, rainforests, golden&nbsp;
savannahs dotted by elephants and lions. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.420
there’s this idea of Africa as a perpetual&nbsp;
humanitarian tragedy - famine, epidemics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:27.660
violent coups and bitter tribal conflicts. Yes,&nbsp;
Africa has all of these. But the problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:33.960
cliches is that they often make you miss the real&nbsp;
story. And the real story is that the world itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:42.300
is becoming more African. Fast. Projections show&nbsp;
that by 2050, Africa’s current population of 1.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.880
billion will double. That means that one out&nbsp;
of every four people on Earth might be African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.400
just thirty years from now. Throughout this vast&nbsp;
continent, country after country is undergoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.980
an enormous youth bulge. In several, more than&nbsp;
half the population would not be old enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:06.300
buy alcohol in the United States. In Uganda the&nbsp;
median age is 15. Some see a future in which this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.000
massive, youthful population propels African&nbsp;
nations onto the global stage in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.520
could mirror the rise of China. But other experts&nbsp;
say that without massive new job opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.740
this rapid growth is a recipe for poverty,&nbsp;
radicalization, and violent conflict.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.980
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It&nbsp;
Matters. Today, the world’s African future.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.320
DW AFRICA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.000
Africa’s population is growing fast.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.204
CNBC INTERNATIONAL: This population boom&nbsp;
will be dominated by one continent, Africa.

00:01:41.204 --> 00:01:43.260
BBC WORLD NEWS: While the boom&nbsp;
in numbers brings opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.620
there are also many challenges like&nbsp;
all those extra mouths to feed.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.440
TED: Have to unleash the genius&nbsp;
of our young people. Get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.980
their way. Support them to create&nbsp;
and innovate, and lead the way.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.180
GITHONGO: I always tell people, the trains run on&nbsp;
time in Switzerland, but I've not heard of notable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.560
thriller writers from Switzerland. The thrillers&nbsp;
are coming out of places that are developing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.260
where everything is changing, it's funky, it's&nbsp;
difficult. And I think that really Africa's future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:17.280
is in its youth. My name is John Githongo,&nbsp;
I am CEO of Inuka Kenya and publisher of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:24.540
Elephant. I've been involved in anti-corruption&nbsp;
work and media for 33 years, around the world.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.720
SIERRA: John is an activist and a journalist who&nbsp;
fled Kenya after exposing government corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.720
and returned in 2008. He spoke with&nbsp;
us over zoom from his home in Nairobi.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.420
SIERRA: So, is that your kids&nbsp;
we hear in the background?

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:44.220
GITHONGO: Well, you know, I live&nbsp;
in an apartment block. And with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.360
the COVID pandemic, the poor guys.&nbsp;
You know, they're out there all day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.260
and sometimes late into the night.&nbsp;
They play out in the front here, so.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.700
SIERRA: Wow, that's lovely.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:01.020
GITHONGO: Yes, absolutely. One of the sad things&nbsp;
that has happened in Africa is because Africa is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.460
so rich in terms of natural resources and remains&nbsp;
the world's richest continent in terms of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.540
you know, looking for gold, or the the&nbsp;
minerals used to make mobile phones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.760
solar panels, they they come out of Africa, this&nbsp;
has been a curse for us. Our primary resource is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.380
our people. Africa is the world's youngest&nbsp;
continent, most rapidly growing population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.148 --> 00:03:25.020
as a net contributor to the world's population&nbsp;
as the other continents sort of are slowing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:31.140
or shrinking in terms of population. We had&nbsp;
1.2 billion people on this continent in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:37.680
and will be, you know, 2.5 billion by 2050. And&nbsp;
that trajectory is going to continue like that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.460
I think. And so yes, people call it you&nbsp;
know, it's a youth bulge. So this new young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:50.040
generation of Africans are very worldly,&nbsp;
very precocious, and much better informed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.100
the best informed generation we’ve&nbsp;
ever had. Citizens of the world.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.780
SIERRA: It’s important to note that in this&nbsp;
episode we’re going to be talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:04.980
Sub-Saharan Africa. Forty-six of Africa’s&nbsp;
fifty-four nations lie entirely or at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.320
partially below the Sahara desert. The world&nbsp;
of international relations tends to think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:16.680
these forty-six nations as a group, whereas the&nbsp;
six nations of North Africa, all members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.900
League of Arab States, are usually thought of as&nbsp;
being part of the Arab world. There are problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.660
with this model, but it is widely used.  
 
GAVIN: In most African countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.660
70% or more of the population is under the age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.400
of 30. So there the whole demographic&nbsp;
profile looks really, really different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.760
Hi, I'm Michelle Gavin. I'm a senior fellow for&nbsp;
Africa, at the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:46.560
SIERRA: From 2011 to 2014, Michelle was&nbsp;
the United States ambassador to Botswana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.900
and served as the United States representative&nbsp;
to the Southern African Development Community.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:56.100
GAVIN: These are youth dominated societies&nbsp;
and that has all kinds of really interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:06.000
implications for politics and economics. We live&nbsp;
in an interdependent world and Africa is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:13.860
be able to sort of assert itself on a global stage&nbsp;
and the concerns of those young Africans whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:19.680
numbers are vast, whose backgrounds are incredibly&nbsp;
varied, whose circumstances are incredibly varied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:26.280
but their concerns are going to find expression in&nbsp;
African foreign policy and in African engagements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:32.100
at international institutions. And both&nbsp;
with these demographic numbers where so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:36.900
much of the world is going to be African, and&nbsp;
the fact that it is 54 countries, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:43.020
a lot of votes in international forae. Things&nbsp;
aren't going to get done without African help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:50.220
and important US priorities aren't going to be&nbsp;
addressed if there's massive African resistance.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:57.300
SIERRA: Okay a whole lot to unpack there!&nbsp;
But maybe a good way is to start local.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:02.940
What does it even look like to walk around&nbsp;
in a place where just everyone is so young?

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:09.180
GAVIN: Yeah, I think, sometimes at least, there's&nbsp;
a certain kind of reporting from the continent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.920
that unfortunately, always seems to start off&nbsp;
with, you know, as I stared out the window of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:21.420
taxi, I noticed groups of young men, you know, on&nbsp;
the street corner. I felt a sense of unease. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:28.620
it's problematic in lots of ways. Right? But, I&nbsp;
think it springs from just people being unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.840
with societies that look so incredibly young.&nbsp;
There's no time you're going to be walking out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.560
the street when you don't notice a lot of groups&nbsp;
of young people, because people are very young.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:45.240
SIERRA: The median age in India is around 27. In&nbsp;
the United States it’s closer to 38. In Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:51.780
it’s around 40. And in Japan it’s closer to&nbsp;
50. Compare that to the median age in Nigeria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:59.280
Africa’s most populous nation, which is 18. Every&nbsp;
country has its own demographic concerns. Japan is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.200
facing a dwindling population that could endanger&nbsp;
its status as one of the world’s most powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:10.380
economies. Africa’s outlook is the opposite,&nbsp;
and the scale of the trend is off the charts.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.800
So why did this boom happen? Is there&nbsp;
a reason that this is happening now?

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.320
GAVIN: Well, so, most of the world has gone&nbsp;
through sort of a demographic transition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.420
but there are sort of generalizations one can&nbsp;
make where societies tend to have higher birth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:34.740
rates at a certain era and usually at the same&nbsp;
time, there's also very high mortality rates. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:39.360
basically, families have lots of children, and&nbsp;
many, many families lose children as well. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.400
then over time, and there's lots of factors that&nbsp;
contribute to this, women's education being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.760
huge one, access to family planning. Over time,&nbsp;
people tend to choose smaller families and sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.800
of the population pyramid, if you can picture&nbsp;
it, with kind of children at the bottom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.120
your most elderly members of society at a point&nbsp;
at the top. Over time that pyramid starts to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.260
instead of being really wide at the base,&nbsp;
you know, sort of get taller and pointier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:09.660
And Africa is sort of at a stage where there’s&nbsp;
still pretty high birth rates, large families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:17.160
but there have been huge advances in health, and&nbsp;
people are living longer, children are surviving.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:23.040
SIERRA: According to the World Bank, in&nbsp;
2018 Sub-Saharan Africa had an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:28.980
fertility rate of 4.6 births per woman.&nbsp;
This has gone down significantly since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.660
the middle of the 20th century, but remains&nbsp;
far higher than in many other parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:39.420
world. At the same time infant mortality&nbsp;
has plummeted and life expectancy has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:44.460
shot up. To put it simply, far more&nbsp;
Africans are being born than dying.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:50.880
GAVIN: This is something that happened in&nbsp;
East Asia right back at the time of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.440
massive economic expansion, and it was something&nbsp;
called the demographic dividend. The idea being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:04.260
suddenly these societies had a huge number of&nbsp;
working age in their prime people. And were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:11.160
to kind of harness that amazing potential to make&nbsp;
these huge economic development gains. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:16.020
innovation all over the continent. And this is one&nbsp;
of the things that doesn't get enough attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:16.980
right? You know, just like any other part of the&nbsp;
world, there's good news and bad, but there are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:23.100
you know, amazing tech entrepreneurs in Nigeria,&nbsp;
in Kenya, there's incredible scientific research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:29.940
that gets done in Dakar in Senegal. There's&nbsp;
a lot. But is there any place that's got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:37.800
a, you know, clear roadmap for how to generate the&nbsp;
jobs of the future, that is definitively going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:43.500
be successful? No, because, you know, there's a&nbsp;
host of unknowns. You know, most African societies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:48.960
do deal with pretty high rates of unemployment&nbsp;
that, that's a little misleading, in that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:54.780
informal sector is incredibly robust in a lot&nbsp;
of African countries. And sometimes, what looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:59.160
like a bunch of society being unemployed just&nbsp;
means that employment’s happening in an informal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:04.620
sector. But there too when you think about the&nbsp;
kind of infrastructure and educational needs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:09.120
these vast growing societies, eventually, you're&nbsp;
going to have to formalize that sector if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:14.100
going to do the sort of revenue generation&nbsp;
that's required. So there's a lot there. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:18.300
you know, different societies, again, of&nbsp;
course, look different, right? Nigeria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:22.500
with its incredibly large population, or&nbsp;
Ethiopia, with its huge population look very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:28.200
different than a Botswana with, you know, about&nbsp;
2 million people in a country the size of France.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:34.560
SIERRA: There are a range of projections for&nbsp;
where Africa’s population boom is headed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.860
and the most common ones tend to be pretty grim.

