radicalize or trying to mobilize southerners&nbsp;
against northerners, or what have you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:46.440
it does create maybe fertile ground for that kind&nbsp;
of destabilizing sort of societal mobilization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:53.520
Then there's the, look at this incredible&nbsp;
potential, look at this vast amount of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:59.880
capital, right? All this kind of latent potential&nbsp;
that just needs to be sort of harnessed to a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:05.940
of economic projects and goals and, and then you&nbsp;
have the incredible, you know, manpower of Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:12.060
finding expression in economic development&nbsp;
and growth. And all of it’s true, right? At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:16.920
the same time. You have all of it, it just really&nbsp;
depends on what specifically one is talking about.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:21.180
GITHONGO: You know, life is complicated.&nbsp;
That's the nature of life, and it's full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:25.860
contradictions. So, it’s a bit of both. In&nbsp;
countries where the the governance systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:31.080
and the leadership is weak, or corrupt, or&nbsp;
incompetent, then it becomes a curse because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:34.620
you're unable to provide for, you know, for&nbsp;
this younger generation that that want jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:38.760
that want an income, that want health, that&nbsp;
want education, and that frustration boils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:42.180
over and causes instability. But&nbsp;
I'd like to think it's a blessing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:47.100
because with youth comes energy, comes&nbsp;
idealism and that's what a society needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:52.740
develop. And the pace of development is quite&nbsp;
unprecedented across the continent. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:59.220
I visit a country and then go there five years&nbsp;
later, and just the level of development in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:03.360
terms of infrastructure, and just what young&nbsp;
people are doing and how they're doing it.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:13.740
GAVIN: So in addition to being the youngest&nbsp;
part of the world, Africa is also the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:19.200
rapidly urbanizing part of the world. And that,&nbsp;
you know, again, a host of things flow from that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:24.720
some exciting, some really challenging. So&nbsp;
there's lots of issues around affordable housing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:31.800
urban infrastructure, water, transportation,&nbsp;
etc. It also has really interesting political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:37.320
implications because it is easier for young&nbsp;
people, particularly young adults to organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:43.380
in an urban environment, it's easier to share&nbsp;
information, it's easier to engage in the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:50.520
of activism that tends to demand a response from&nbsp;
authorities. And so we have seen a huge uptick in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:55.700
demonstrations, in peaceful demonstrations&nbsp;
in a host of different African societies.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:19:01.740
GITHONGO: And in the next 20 or so years,&nbsp;
some of the world's largest cities will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:08.520
in Africa. Lagos, Dar es Salaam, etc, will&nbsp;
be giant mega cities with 10s of millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:13.740
people living in them. But across the rest of&nbsp;
the country, we have gigantic cities that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:19.500
growing very quickly, organically, and a lot&nbsp;
of the trade is increasingly within Africa.

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:22.560
SIERRA: And as Africa continues to urbanize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:26.820
goods and services are not the&nbsp;
only thing flowing across borders.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:34.980
Truly the one thing that jumped in my mind first,&nbsp;
when I heard just about a continent full of very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:40.260
young people is that the art, the music, the&nbsp;
literature, the culture, it must be so incredible.

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:46.260
GAVIN: There's so much going on. Absolutely.&nbsp;
The filmmaking, the literature. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:50.580
there's a lot coming from Africa that&nbsp;
I feel like we're starting to get more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:55.520
of a taste of in our own kind of cultural&nbsp;
diet. But there's a lot we're missing, too.

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:01.800
GITHONGO: And for this young generation of&nbsp;
Africans keep in mind, they are borderless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:08.940
This young generation is very connected to the&nbsp;
African American community to the afro-Brazilian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:13.560
community. Combine the fact that, you know,&nbsp;
especially in the arts and culture and language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:19.380
this is one community, and increasingly think&nbsp;
like that. And so there's a new sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:25.620
African sensibility and sense of Africanness.&nbsp;
And they're able to connect into global movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:31.320
and zeitgeists with great ease and quite&nbsp;
seamlessly than the previous generations.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:35.880
SIERRA: And of course, shifting cultural views is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:38.580
just one part of what these&nbsp;
new generations care about.

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:44.220
SIERRA: I mean, do you foresee&nbsp;
big political shifts? As you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.800
these young people demand more say?

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:53.760
GAVIN: Absolutely. I think, as young, particularly&nbsp;
urban Africans, demand more from their government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:21:02.100
So more accountability, more service delivery,&nbsp;
and more of a sense of voice and agency in where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:07.620
their government’s going. I think it changes the&nbsp;
nature of politics in a really interesting way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.620 --> 00:21:15.060
And I think that it comes, you know, at a time&nbsp;
where, in part because of its demographic might,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:21.060
we're seeing African countries be more assertive&nbsp;
on the world stage anyway. So I think this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:27.540
sort of idea of African agency, both at the&nbsp;
domestic level in Africa's many democracies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:34.080
and at an international level in terms of African&nbsp;
engagement with international institutions, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:38.880
think this is kind of the big trend that's coming,&nbsp;
that everybody's going to have to reckon with.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:44.040
SIERRA: And what we’re seeing in this&nbsp;
borderless interconnected world of arts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:48.240
culture, and politics, is that some&nbsp;
countries are rushing in to invest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:51.720
Of these outside investors, the largest is China.

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:56.880
GAVIN: You know, if you fly into an African&nbsp;
airport, the chances are pretty good that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:01.320
was built by the Chinese, that the road you're&nbsp;
driving on was built by the Chinese, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:07.380
port you pass is built by the Chinese. There's&nbsp;
been tremendous investments in infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:12.300
and particularly very kind of tangible&nbsp;
and visible infrastructure and, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:18.660
parliamentary buildings, the things that everyone&nbsp;
can see. So the Chinese have been engaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.660 --> 00:22:22.800
throughout the continent for a very, very long&nbsp;
time, they're the most important trading partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:28.920
to the African continent. They provide financing&nbsp;
for a lot of these big projects. They used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:34.620
provide a lot of the labor, that is less true as&nbsp;
there's been pushback from African societies. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:40.260
the Chinese are tremendously engaged in seeking&nbsp;
political influence on the continent and present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:47.760
themselves very specifically as a contrast to&nbsp;
Western partners. There's a big emphasis still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:51.600
on non-interference in, in others&nbsp;
political affairs, and they point to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:57.480
their own, you know, dramatic economic growth,&nbsp;
and their impressive development story as sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:03.180
of one to be emulated. And, you know, it comes&nbsp;
with this other package of political values.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:10.860
GITHONGO: China commercially, is now Africa's&nbsp;
most significant partner. And that has grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:16.380
exponentially of course, they want the raw&nbsp;
materials, but they also need a market for what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:21.480
they’re producing. As, you know, the markets in&nbsp;
the West become more and more difficult. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:28.080
there’s trade trade challenges with the US, for&nbsp;
example. So there's a lot more anxiety about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:32.220
China in the West, than there is in Africa.&nbsp;
There are problems, people like me who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:36.480
involved in anti corruption work are very&nbsp;
critical of the fact that yes, they enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:42.060
into very significant public works contracts. So&nbsp;
we've seen a lot of a lot of building of roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:46.620
stadiums, schools, has improved infrastructure&nbsp;
tremendously. There's been a lot of corruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.620 --> 00:23:50.580
accompanying it, partly because the contracts&nbsp;
are very opaque, and their system doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:55.680
allow any interrogation of how these contracts&nbsp;
entered into, of kickbacks with pay, it's very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.680 --> 00:24:00.240
very difficult to establish. We've acquired&nbsp;
a huge amount of commercial debt from China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:04.320
which is causing us tremendous problems in&nbsp;
a number of countries and that that day of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.320 --> 00:24:09.360
reckoning is coming. But I don't think it's&nbsp;
a relationship that is going to slow down. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:15.360
see same thing with India, Turkey, Iran. All&nbsp;
of a sudden they're very present in Africa.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:19.800
SIERRA: You know, I was listening to the&nbsp;
countries you were listing there: India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:23.160
Turkey, Iran, but I did not hear you say the U.S.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:30.960
GITHONGO: You know, it is- the US is, at&nbsp;
once, hugely inspiring. Tremendous soft power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.960 --> 00:24:34.860
people want to be like the US. Many of&nbsp;
the constitutions that have been written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.140
in Africa since the 1990s after the fall of the&nbsp;
Berlin Wall, resemble the U the US Constitution.

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:48.000
The US’ own political system, which seems&nbsp;
to be based on a gridlock kind of approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:52.140
to politics. So you have multiple&nbsp;
checks and balances, which is good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:56.880
but it means that very often, you know, at the&nbsp;
last minute, the government has no funding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:56.880 --> 00:25:00.180
the government and the congressmen and&nbsp;
senators have to sit down and agree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:04.980
it seems to have been designed by the founding&nbsp;
fathers deliberately to force people to to to sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:09.900
together and agree and we're seeing that system&nbsp;
fraying. Particularly over the last few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:16.320
with President Trump being in office and what has&nbsp;
happened to the Republican Party and social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:22.620
which has shown very startling the treatment&nbsp;
of people of color. That has caused a change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:26.880
in consciousness about the US. So for the first&nbsp;
time, you know, you have, you know, the dream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:31.500
every parent is to get your child was to get the&nbsp;
child educated in American Ivy League University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:37.800
not so much anymore. We have a lot more students&nbsp;
going off to India, to China, even now to Turkey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:44.400
So US is still a hugely important partner to&nbsp;
Africa, not so much commercially. The commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:50.100
relationship between Africa and the US is minor&nbsp;
compared to the relationship with India or China.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:55.920
SIERRA: And how young Africans see the&nbsp;
U.S. is important, because they are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.920 --> 00:26:00.060
ones who are going to make choices about the&nbsp;
relationships their countries make with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:04.320
rest of the world. Whether they will want&nbsp;
to reforge a bond with the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:09.000
or continue to embrace the ready&nbsp;
investments of China and other countries.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:16.640
Do you think that the US is doing a good&nbsp;
job of forging partnerships in Africa?

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:24.060
GAVIN: So I think that there are many very&nbsp;
capable public servants and private citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:29.640
doing good work, but no. I think I think that&nbsp;
the Trump administration has been kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:36.180
disastrous for the US image in Africa. The set&nbsp;
of unforced errors, kind of insulting language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:44.880
dismissive diplomatic approaches. I don't think&nbsp;
that the US private sector still is as engaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:49.680
as makes sense in trying to seize African&nbsp;
opportunities. So when I was in government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:54.240
often hear lots of complaints about,&nbsp;
you know, the Chinese are eating our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:58.620
lunch. But sometimes when there were&nbsp;
major tenders, major opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:04.380
you wouldn't even have any American firms bidding.&nbsp;
So I don't know how you can complain about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:11.340
Chinese or pretend that this is all because of&nbsp;
corruption, when no one even was in the mix. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:17.100
there is a tremendous amount of work to be done.&nbsp;
And I do think it starts with exactly what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:22.800
whole conversation’s been about. It starts with&nbsp;
Americans getting more aware, more educated.

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:27.600
SIERRA: Because it does seem like this,&nbsp;
with this much at play, there are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:32.400
strategic and economic opportunities to&nbsp;
be a constructive part of Africa's boom.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:37.980
GAVIN: Absolutely, but it requires,&nbsp;
you know, acknowledging kind of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:42.240
African interests are and looking for&nbsp;
that overlap in the Venn diagram with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:47.520
our interests. As opposed to imagining&nbsp;
this vast continent as some kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:52.080
you know, gameboard for your geopolitical&nbsp;
competition with some other major power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:59.340
or thinking of Africa as some kind of extra&nbsp;
credit altruistic exercise, which is absurd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:05.340
right, and leads to, you know, some very poorly&nbsp;
thought out types of engagements and initiatives.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:11.640
SIERRA: So do you think that African&nbsp;
nations need help from the outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:16.220
to handle this well? And if so, what&nbsp;
type of help? What's the right help?

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:23.160
GAVIN: I think that, yes, in the sense that&nbsp;
there are big challenges that no one country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:30.060
can possibly address alone. What I don't&nbsp;
think is that what's required is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:37.620
a bunch of consultants sort of parachuting&nbsp;
in to design jobs programs, or, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:46.020
I love entrepreneurs too, and Africa is full of&nbsp;
exciting entrepreneurial stories. But if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:52.260
at the sheer numbers of what we're talking about,&nbsp;
right, you're not going to entrepreneur your way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:56.520
out of this problem of how to create these jobs,&nbsp;
right? Everybody isn’t going to start their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.520 --> 00:29:02.220
business. That's insane. That's not the kind&nbsp;
of numbers we're talking about in terms of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.220 --> 00:29:08.220
number of people who need work, work that is going&nbsp;
to provide enough for them to have a dignified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:14.580
life and provide for their families and afford&nbsp;
that. So I think there's a global conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:19.200
that people have been having about the future&nbsp;
of work that very strangely, rarely seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:24.360
include Africa. It's nuts to me that people sit&nbsp;
down in Davos to talk about the future of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:29.640
and nobody's speaking from the point of view of&nbsp;
the, the part of the world that's going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:34.380
the largest labor force. You know, when there&nbsp;
are these pat answers around entrepreneurship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:42.300
around leapfrogging technologies. Some things&nbsp;
can't be lept over, right, like roads, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:49.620
ports, and sort of the infrastructure that China&nbsp;
tends to fund. That is, you know, it's just an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:55.200
essential part of what's going to have to happen.&nbsp;
So, you know, that kind of financing that China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.200 --> 00:30:00.180
provides that the rest of the world by and large,&nbsp;
does not, is going to continue to be needed.

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:07.620
GITHONGO: The most profound reality facing&nbsp;
Africa is demographic. That's why I was keen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.620 --> 00:30:12.420
to participate in this conversation with you,&nbsp;
because I think the issue that you've chosen is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:17.640
the defining issue for Africa. Is, what do, you&nbsp;
know, is how, what happens to this generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:22.740
that is so young, and is growing so fast, is&nbsp;
going to determine the future of Africa. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:28.980
it will be determined by what Africans decide they&nbsp;
want for themselves, plus, the relationships that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:32.400
are now the old relationships that&nbsp;
are being rekindled with the East,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:39.840
etc. and ties with the West that are now sort of&nbsp;
just basically, it's not that they're being cut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:45.000
but it’s simply that other nations are&nbsp;
rising. And as the world changes, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:50.180
we're able to take advantage of that for our own&nbsp;
political, economic, and social benefit is key.

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:55.620
SIERRA: Whether this youthful energy is&nbsp;
harnessed for innovation and economic progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.620 --> 00:31:02.220
or whether it spirals into unrest, poverty&nbsp;
and radicalization, remains to be seen. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:08.400
it’s sheer size will inevitably impact all of&nbsp;
us. Some nations have long since realized this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:14.460
and are moving quickly to invest in Africa and&nbsp;
influence its course. Others, like our own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:20.940
are moving slowly, and as a result may lose out&nbsp;
on forming a fresh bond with a population that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:27.480
could one day make up one third of humanity.&nbsp;
Africa’s power will exert itself economically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:33.720
it will exert itself with its mass of votes in&nbsp;
international bodies like the UN, it will be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:39.480
essential ally in combating global challenges&nbsp;
like epidemics and Climate Change.Africa’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:47.100
boom is inevitable. It is already happening.&nbsp;
The question is this: will we be part of it?

